      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine after how many bounces the ball first rises to a height less than 30 cm, we start by analyzing the height of each bounce. The ball is initially dropped from a height of 243 cm. After the first bounce, it rises to \\( \\frac{2}{3} \\times 243 \\) cm. After the second bounce, it rises to \\( \\frac{2}{3} \\times \\frac{2}{3} \\times 243 \\) cm, and so on. The height after the \\( n \\)-th bounce is given by:\n\n\\[\nh_n = 243 \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^n\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest integer \\( n \\) such that \\( h_n < 30 \\). Therefore, we set up the inequality:\n\n\\[\n243 \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^n < 30\n\\]\n\nFirst, we divide both sides by 243:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^n < \\frac{30}{243}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{30}{243} = \\frac{10}{81}\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^n < \\frac{10}{81}\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( n \\), we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n\\[\n\\ln \\left( \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^n \\right) < \\ln \\left( \\frac{10}{81} \\right)\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of logarithms \\( \\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a) \\), we get:\n\n\\[\nn \\ln \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right) < \\ln \\left( \\frac{10}{81} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSince \\( \\ln \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right) \\) is negative, we divide both sides by \\( \\ln \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right) \\) and reverse the inequality:\n\n\\[\nn > \\frac{\\ln \\left( \\frac{10}{81} \\right)}{\\ln \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)}\n\\]\n\nCalculate the values of the logarithms:\n\n\\[\n\\ln \\left( \\frac{10}{81} \\right) = \\ln(10) - \\ln(81) \\approx 2.3026 - 4.3945 = -2.0919\n\\]\n\\[\n\\ln \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right) \\approx -0.4055\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\nn > \\frac{-2.0919}{-0.4055} \\approx 5.16\n\\]\n\nSince \\( n \\) must be an integer, we round up to the next whole number:\n\n\\[\nn = 6\n\\]\n\nTo confirm, we calculate the height after 5 and 6 bounces:\n\nAfter 5 bounces:\n\n\\[\nh_5 = 243 \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^5 = 243 \\times \\frac{32}{243} = 32 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nAfter 6 bounces:\n\n\\[\nh_6 = 243 \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^6 = 243 \\times \\frac{64}{729} = 21.33 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\nSince 32 cm is greater than 30 cm and 21.33 cm is less than 30 cm, the ball first rises to a height less than 30 cm after 6 bounces. Therefore, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
