      "text": "Let's denote the height of the ball after the \\(n\\)-th bounce by \\(h_n\\). Initially, the ball is dropped from a height of 243 cm, so \\(h_0 = 243\\) cm.\n\nEach time the ball bounces, it reaches a height that is \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\) of the height from which it fell. Therefore, we can express the height after the \\(n\\)-th bounce as:\n\\[ h_n = 243 \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest integer \\(n\\) such that \\(h_n < 30\\) cm. This gives us the inequality:\n\\[ 243 \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n < 30 \\]\n\nFirst, we isolate \\(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n\\):\n\\[ \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n < \\frac{30}{243} \\]\n\\[ \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n < \\frac{10}{81} \\]\n\nTo solve for \\(n\\), we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\\[ \\ln \\left(\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n\\right) < \\ln \\left(\\frac{10}{81}\\right) \\]\n\\[ n \\ln \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) < \\ln \\left(\\frac{10}{81}\\right) \\]\n\nSince \\(\\ln \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\) is negative, we divide both sides by \\(\\ln \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)\\) and reverse the inequality:\n\\[ n > \\frac{\\ln \\left(\\frac{10}{81}\\right)}{\\ln \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} \\]\n\nNow, we calculate the logarithms:\n\\[ \\ln \\left(\\frac{10}{81}\\right) = \\ln(10) - \\ln(81) = \\ln(10) - \\ln(3^4) = \\ln(10) - 4 \\ln(3) \\]\n\\[ \\ln \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) = \\ln(2) - \\ln(3) \\]\n\nSo,\n\\[ n > \\frac{\\ln(10) - 4 \\ln(3)}{\\ln(2) - \\ln(3)} \\]\n\nUsing approximate values \\(\\ln(10) \\approx 2.3026\\), \\(\\ln(3) \\approx 1.0986\\), and \\(\\ln(2) \\approx 0.6931\\), we get:\n\\[ n > \\frac{2.3026 - 4 \\cdot 1.0986}{0.6931 - 1.0986} = \\frac{2.3026 - 4.3944}{-0.4055} = \\frac{-2.0918}{-0.4055} \\approx 5.16 \\]\n\nSince \\(n\\) must be an integer, we round up to the next whole number:\n\\[ n = 6 \\]\n\nTherefore, the ball first rises less than 30 cm after \\(\\boxed{6}\\) bounces.",
