      "text": "To determine after how many bounces the ball first rises to a height less than 30 cm, we start by analyzing the height of the ball after each bounce. The ball is dropped from a height of 243 cm and bounces back to \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\) of the height from which it fell.\n\nLet's denote the height of the ball after the \\(n\\)-th bounce as \\(h_n\\). The initial height is \\(h_0 = 243\\) cm. The height after the first bounce is:\n\\[ h_1 = 243 \\times \\frac{2}{3} = 162 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nThe height after the second bounce is:\n\\[ h_2 = 162 \\times \\frac{2}{3} = 108 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nThe height after the third bounce is:\n\\[ h_3 = 108 \\times \\frac{2}{3} = 72 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nThe height after the fourth bounce is:\n\\[ h_4 = 72 \\times \\frac{2}{3} = 48 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nThe height after the fifth bounce is:\n\\[ h_5 = 48 \\times \\frac{2}{3} = 32 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nThe height after the sixth bounce is:\n\\[ h_6 = 32 \\times \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{64}{3} \\approx 21.33 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nThe height after the seventh bounce is:\n\\[ h_7 = \\frac{64}{3} \\times \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{128}{9} \\approx 14.22 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nThe height after the eighth bounce is:\n\\[ h_8 = \\frac{128}{9} \\times \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{256}{27} \\approx 9.49 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nThe height after the ninth bounce is:\n\\[ h_9 = \\frac{256}{27} \\times \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{512}{81} \\approx 6.34 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nThe height after the tenth bounce is:\n\\[ h_{10} = \\frac{512}{81} \\times \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{1024}{243} \\approx 4.22 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nWe see that after the eighth bounce, the height is approximately 9.49 cm, which is less than 30 cm. Therefore, the ball first rises to a height less than 30 cm after the eighth bounce.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{8}\n\\]",
